  "body": "## Description\n```solidity\n    function _updateLimitPostOrder(Book storage self, Limit storage limit, Order memory order) private {\n        limit.numOrders++;\n\n\n        if (limit.headOrder.isNull()) {\n            limit.headOrder = order.id;\n            limit.tailOrder = order.id;\n        } else {\n            Order storage tailOrder = self.orders[limit.tailOrder];\n            tailOrder.nextOrderId = order.id;\n            order.prevOrderId = tailOrder.id;\n            limit.tailOrder = order.id;\n        }\n```\nIn `BookLib::_updateLimitPostOrder`, when appending a new order to an existing limit, the code correctly updates the previous tail’s `nextOrderId` and sets `order.prevOrderId = tailOrder.id` on the in-memory Order struct but ir  never writes that updated `order` back into storage. Because order lives only in memory at that point, its prevOrderId is never persisted. As a result, every newly added order ends up with a zero prevOrderId , breaking the doubly-linked list succession.\n\n## Impact\nWith this bug, a limit list is correctly linked in the `next` direction but completely broken in the `prev` one. When a middle order of this list is removed (canceled/amended/filled) the code that modifies the list is `_updateLimitRemoveOrder` which does:\n```solidity\n        OrderId prev = order.prevOrderId;\n        OrderId next = order.nextOrderId;\n\n\n        if (!prev.isNull()) self.orders[prev].nextOrderId = next;\n        else limit.headOrder = next;\n\n\n        if (!next.isNull()) self.orders[next].prevOrderId = prev;\n        else limit.tailOrder = prev;\n```\n\nwith the broken prev reference, we enter the first else branch meaning that the headOrder is overwritten with `order.next`. An example scenario (also given in the PoC) is:\n\n- order3 (tail) <-> order2 <-> order1 (head)\n\n- remove(order2)\n\n- order3 (tail) (head)   | we completely lose track of order1, which becomes unfillable.",
  "proof_of_concept": "paste this code in `PoC.t.sol` and run it with `forge test --match-test submissionValidity -vv`\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\n\nimport {PoCTestBase} from \"./PoCTestBase.t.sol\";\n\nimport {Side, Order} from \"contracts/clob/types/Order.sol\";\nimport {Limit} from \"contracts/clob/types/Book.sol\";\nimport {ICLOB} from \"contracts/clob/ICLOB.sol\";\nimport {CLOB} from \"contracts/clob/CLOB.sol\";\n\n\ncontract PoC is PoCTestBase {\n    function test_submissionValidity() external {\n        address user = users[0];\n        setupTokens(Side.BUY, user, 10 ether, 1 ether, true);\n\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory args = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: 1 ether,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: 1 ether,\n            cancelTimestamp: 0,\n            side: Side.BUY,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        });\n\n        vm.startPrank(user);\n        //  order1 (head) (tail)\n        uint256 orderId1 = clob.postLimitOrder(user, args).orderId;\n        //  order2 (tail) <-> order1 (head)\n        uint256 orderId2 = clob.postLimitOrder(user, args).orderId;\n        // order3 (tail) <-> order2 <-> order1 (head)\n        uint256 orderId3 = clob.postLimitOrder(user, args).orderId;\n\n        Order memory order1 = clob.getOrder(orderId1);\n        Order memory order2 = clob.getOrder(orderId2);\n        Order memory order3 = clob.getOrder(orderId3);\n\n        assertEq(order2.prevOrderId.unwrap(), 0); // order2 doesn't have the pointer to order1!!!\n        assertEq(order3.prevOrderId.unwrap(), 0); // order3 doesn't have the pointer to order3!!!\n\n        uint256[] memory orderIds = new uint256[](1);\n        orderIds[0] = orderId2;\n        ICLOB.CancelArgs memory argsCancel = ICLOB.CancelArgs({orderIds: orderIds});\n        clob.cancel(user, argsCancel);\n\n        // after we cancel order2, the headOrder should still be order1 however it becomes order3\n        Limit memory limit = clob.getLimit(1 ether, Side.BUY);\n        assertEq(limit.headOrder.unwrap(), orderId3);\n\n        // since order3 is now both head and tail, if we remove order3 too the limit becomes empty,\n        // effectively making the original headOrder (order1) impossible to match\n\n        orderIds[0] = orderId3;\n        argsCancel = ICLOB.CancelArgs({orderIds: orderIds});\n        clob.cancel(user, argsCancel);\n        limit = clob.getLimit(1 ether, Side.BUY);\n        assertEq(limit.headOrder.unwrap(), 0);\n        assertEq(limit.tailOrder.unwrap(), 0);\n    }\n}\n\n```",
